WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
in what are called 
“honour killings”.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.800
Israa Ghrayeb

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.200
Qandeel Baloch 

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.880
and the many others
who’ve died

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.320
in what are called 
“honour killings”.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:13.560
It’s thousands of women  

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.520
murdered around
the world every year.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.680
So what is an honour killing?

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.760
Why is it still happening? 

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.320
And how can these
 murders be stopped? 

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.480
It’s tough to even use the 
term honour killings

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.600
because there’s nothing honourable about women 

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.800
being killed to 
supposedly save face.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.600
We’re going to look into why it’s happening in a bit.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.880
But first let’s 
rewind to a murder  

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.800
that got thousands
 of people protesting.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.080
It happened in Jordan.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.040
Witnesses said they 
saw a young woman  

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.720
being chased out of her 
house by her father

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:05.000
who then beat her to death with a brick in the street.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.960
Some in the neighbourhood reportedly saw him sit down

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.960
and drink tea next to 
his daughter’s body.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.000
We know that the victim 
was a divorced woman  

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.520
in her late 30s.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.440
She had been living 
in her family home 

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:19.240
but at one point she went 
to a women’s facility

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.840
saying she was experiencing domestic abuse.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.920
A few days later she was back at her parents house 

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.040
and killed not long afterwards.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.520
According to the 
United Nations 

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.040
50,000 women around the world were killed 

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.000
by their intimate partners or family members in 2017. 

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.360
Honour killings make up a part of those numbers.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:06.440
The UN estimates it’s about 5,000 every year.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:10.240
And Ahlam’s murder is just a recent high-profile case.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.600
In 2019 in Palestine 21-year
 old Israa Ghrayeb

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.080
was thrown off a balcony because she was seen 

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.040
in public with her fiance.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.560
Israa survived the fall 
only to be killed 

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.520
while she was in hospital by her father and brother.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.680
A nurse recorded this video. 

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:33.880
You can hear Israa 
screaming for help.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.800
In India it was the 
caste system 

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.280
that nearly killed 
Kausalya Shankar. 

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.640
She and her husband were beaten in broad daylight 

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.360
by her family because he was from a lower caste. 

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.680
She survived but he died.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.680
And then there was Saba, a 19-year-old from Pakistan

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.320
who was shot in the face
 and thrown into a river  

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:55.880
by her father and uncle 
for marrying a man

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.080
without her family’s approval.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.440
Saba survived.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.560
And her story featured in the Academy Award-winning

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.960
documentary Girl in the River.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.000
We spoke to the film’s director Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.160
and asked her what
 drives these men

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.360
to kill members of 
their own family.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:33.760
And that construct of honour is often attached

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.160
to a woman’s body 
or her behaviour.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.520
And some men claim 
  that loss of honour

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.560
in the family and 
the communityi55

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:08.800
can only be restored by
 killing the woman.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.800
And it’s not just women 
who are targeted.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.280
Men and LGBTQ people are also murdered. 

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.360
It’s just not as common.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:31.120
So why are they 
still happening?

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:34.680
Activists say it has a lot to do with changing times.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.760
More young people are asserting themselves  

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:38.080
and their rights.

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:53.240
It’s what happened with Qandeel Baloch in 2016.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:05:00.600
She was a 26-year-old social media star in Pakistan.  

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.360
Her killer was her brother.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:23.200
And those structures are based on old concepts

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.080
about ownership that 
go way back.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.960
So how do we change 
that mindset?

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:56.640
Well activists say one of the ways is to change the laws.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.240
In Jordan a killer can get
 their sentence reduced

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:02.160
if a settlement is reached 
with the victim’s family.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:05.120
Thing is the killer 
is the victim’s family.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:08.200
A sentence can also be taken down to just six months

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:11.080
if the killing happened 
in a “fit of fury”.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:13.440
So activists want 
those laws gone

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.760
not only to bring 
justice to victims 

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:18.000
but to stop the crimes happening at all.

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:32.040
Some outdated laws have recently been ditched.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:35.440
In 2017 Jordan repealed a 
law that allowed rapists 

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:38.600
to escape punishment if they married their victims.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:42.120
It’s a first step that sends 
a strong message.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:00.040
And it’s that kind of work 

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:03.200
raising awareness and lobbying for better laws

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.840
that campaigners say 
will save lives.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:09.360
And they’re not prepared to wait for generations.

